---
/**
 * Crude Monte Carlo against importance sampling on the Gaussian tail, where the
 * answer is known to machine precision. The poster is inlined SVG so it stays
 * sharp, keeps its labels as text, and costs no extra request.
 */
import poster from '../generated/raretail-poster.svg?raw';

const label =
  'Plot of the Gaussian tail probability P(Z greater than a) against the ' +
  'threshold a, on a logarithmic vertical axis spanning one down to ten to the ' +
  'minus thirteen. A dashed curve gives the exact value. Filled markers are ' +
  'importance-sampling estimates with plus or minus one standard error, and ' +
  'they track the exact curve across the whole range. Hollow markers are crude ' +
  'Monte Carlo estimates, which stop appearing past a threshold of about five ' +
  'because no sample ever lands in the tail. Color encodes each estimate' +
  "'s relative standard error.";
---

<figure class="figure">
  <div class="figure-frame raretail" data-sim="raretail" role="img" aria-label={label}>
    <div class="poster-svg" data-poster set:html={poster} />
    <canvas data-layer="plot" hidden aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
    <div class="readout" data-readout aria-hidden="true">
      <span>n&nbsp;=&nbsp;<b data-n>8.0M</b></span>
      <span>crude&nbsp;→&nbsp;<b data-crude>3e-7</b></span>
      <span>IS&nbsp;→&nbsp;<b data-is>1e-12</b></span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <figcaption class="figure-caption">
    <b>Fig. 4.</b> The mechanism, on a target with a known answer. Estimating
    <span class="nowrap">P(Z &gt; a)</span> for <span class="nowrap">Z ~ 𝒩(0,1)</span> by crude
    Monte Carlo (hollow) and by importance sampling under the tilted law
    <span class="nowrap">𝒩(a, 1)</span> with weight <span class="nowrap">exp(−aX + a²/2)</span>
    (filled), against the exact <span class="nowrap">Φ(−a)</span>, dashed. Both carry ±1 s.e.
    Watch crude Monte Carlo degrade rather than fail cleanly: its markers drift off the
    curve, go bright as the relative error climbs, survive on a single chance hit two
    decades out of place with an error bar spanning the axis, and then stop appearing.
    The tilted estimator tracks the exact curve five decades further down. The readout
    reports where each method last held a relative standard error under 50%. The
    reference comes from an erf series and the Laplace continued fraction, agreeing
    with SciPy to <span class="nowrap">7×10⁻¹⁵</span>.
  </figcaption>
  <div class="legend" aria-hidden="true">
    <span>10⁻³</span><span class="legend-ramp"></span><span>1</span>
    <span class="legend-note">Color: relative standard error</span>
  </div>
</figure>

<style>
  .raretail { aspect-ratio: 2 / 1; }
  .raretail .readout {
    bottom: auto; left: auto; top: 0; right: 0;
    border-top: 0; border-right: 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-left: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .raretail .poster-svg { display: block; line-height: 0; }
  .raretail :global(svg) { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; }
  .nowrap { white-space: nowrap; }
  .legend-note { margin-left: 0.35rem; }
</style>

<script>
  import '../scripts/raretail.client.js';
</script>
